//? Title: Fisher–Yates: exact choice counting and reproducible seeded shuffles
//? Subtitle: Actual swap traces, the original six-value experiment and a flat-marginal counterexample
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A shuffle must rearrange a list without losing or duplicating elements; the stronger goal
is to make every labelled ordering equally likely. Fisher–Yates achieves that goal under
independent uniform bounded draws by finalising one position at a time. This report inspects
actual native swap records, counts every choice for three elements, retains the original
six-value seeded experiment, and shows why near-flat position frequencies cannot prove
uniformity of entire permutations. The native seed interface is deterministic and uses
a finite-state generator; its reproducibility is a different property from the ideal theorem.
)

\toc

> The method: shrink the range after each finalised position

Starting at i=n−1 and stopping at i=1, draw j uniformly from the inclusive interval [0,i]
and swap the values at i and j. Position i is then final: later stages touch only its prefix.
Self-swaps are allowed. There are n−1 stages for n≥1, and none for an empty list.
The implementation copies its input, then performs those in-place swaps on the owned copy.

With distinguishable input labels, each desired final value at position n−1 selects
one of n original labels. Conditional on that selection, there is one required choice
among n−1 remaining labels for the next position, and so on. Consequently,

$$
P(\text{a specified labelled ordering})
=\frac1n\frac1{n-1}\cdots\frac12=\frac1{n!}.
$$
\alttext(Under independent uniform choices, a specified labelled permutation has probability one over n factorial.)

Equivalently, the shrinking choice tree has n! leaves and a bijection to labelled
permutations. Repeated values do not invalidate the positional algorithm, but different
labelled permutations can look identical after labels are replaced by equal values.

The native sampler is the existing SplitMix64 engine with uniform_u64(0,i), using
multiply-and-reject bounded draws. It is not a modulo reduction. A fixed input and
64-bit seed give a fixed output. Treating consecutive seeds as independent ideal choices
would add an assumption that this reproducible interface does not establish.

> A hand-checkable three-value trace

For [1,2,3], the actual seed-42 run first chooses i=2,j=2, a self-swap that finalises
value 3. Next it chooses i=1,j=0 and finalises value 1. The remaining value is 2.
The result is therefore [2,1,3]. The table contains values read by the shared native
kernel immediately before each swap, rather than a report-local replay.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fy_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
const auto t=dm::algorithms::fisher_yates_shuffle_trace({1,2,3},42);
  std::print("| i | accepted j | value at i before | value at j before | raw words |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto&s:t.steps)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",s.i,s.j,s.at_i,s.at_j,s.random_words);
  std::print("\nActual output: {} {} {}; stages {}; different-position swaps {}; raw words {}.\n",t.outcome.values[0],t.outcome.values[1],t.outcome.values[2],t.outcome.stages,t.outcome.nontrivial_swaps,t.outcome.random_words);
  dm::graphics::Diagram g(60,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle b;b.width=3.6;b.height=1.8;b.font_size=23;
  g.node("input",{0,0},"Input: 1 2 3\nAll positions open",b).node("first",{5.4,0},std::format("i = {}, j = {}\nFix value {}",t.steps[0].i,t.steps[0].j,t.steps[0].at_j),b).node("second",{10.8,0},std::format("i = {}, j = {}\nFix value {}",t.steps[1].i,t.steps[1].j,t.steps[1].at_j),b).edge("input","first","3 choices").edge("first","second","2 choices").text({5.4,-2.3},std::format("Final output: {} {} {}",t.outcome.values[0],t.outcome.values[1],t.outcome.values[2]),28).text({5.4,-3.7},"Later swaps leave the finalised suffix alone",24).save_svg("build/fy_tiny.svg");
  if(t.outcome.values!=std::vector<int>{2,1,3})throw std::runtime_error("tiny trace");
}
