> Finite seeds and the limits of the experiment

A deterministic function of a 64-bit seed has at most 2^64 distinct outputs. Already
21!=51,090,942,171,709,440,000 exceeds 2^64=18,446,744,073,709,551,616, so a 21-element
shuffle initialised only by this seed cannot reach every labelled permutation.
Moreover, for n≥3, n! has a factor of three and cannot divide 2^64. Even an exhaustive
uniform choice among all 64-bit seeds cannot split equally among every permutation.
This does not contradict the ideal theorem, whose independent uniform bounded choices
are a different probability space.

The native seed census counts a nonwrapping interval of consecutive seeds. Its cap
uses C=n²+trials(n+1), accounting for the complete position matrix and execution/label
entries. Whole-output map comparisons and allocations add overhead: this cap is an
admission bound, not an exact operation count. The exact-choice census substitutes
n!, n^n or max(n,1) for trials. Container, label and count-overflow checks remain in
force when optional caps are disabled. Zero trials have an empty whole-output map;
an empty input has one empty possible ordering, counted once per actual execution.

The tiny choice census establishes the bijection for that finite ideal model.
The seed experiment establishes reproducible counts for the specified engine and
seed interval. The rotation control establishes that position marginals do not
identify a joint permutation distribution. These are useful, separate claims.
No external dataset is needed: labelled permutations are the direct objects of study.

> References

- R. Durstenfeld (1964), [Algorithm 235: Random permutation](https://doi.org/10.1145/364520.364540), Communications of the ACM 7(7),420. The citation is verified; the executable descending form is also given in Lemire's Algorithm 1 below.
- P. E. Black, [Fisher-Yates shuffle](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/fisherYatesShuffle.html), NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures, updated 2023. Describes linear-time shrinking ranges and the finite-generator support limitation.
- P. J. Haas (2020), [Correctness Proof for Fisher-Yates Shuffle](https://people.cs.umass.edu/~phaas/CS590M/handouts/Fisher-Yates-proof.pdf). Conditional selection probabilities give the ideal permutation law.
- D. Lemire (2019), [Fast Random Integer Generation in an Interval](https://arxiv.org/html/1805.10941), ACM Transactions on Modeling and Computer Simulation 29(1), article 3, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/3230636). Algorithm 1 gives the shuffle; the range-reduction analysis explains the shared multiply-and-reject sampler.

